# Find Peak Element

Approach

Step 1

Initialize two pointers, left and right, at the beginning and end of the array.

Step 2

Find the middle index.

Step 3

Compare nums[mid] with nums[mid + 1].

Step 4

If nums[mid] is smaller than nums[mid + 1], the array is increasing, so a peak must exist on the right side. Move left to mid + 1.

Step 5

Otherwise, the array is decreasing, so the peak is either at mid or on the left side. Move right to mid.

Step 6

Repeat until left becomes equal to right.

Step 7

Return left because it points to a peak element.

Logic

If nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1], we are moving uphill, so there must be a peak on the right.

If nums[mid] > nums[mid + 1], we are moving downhill, so the current element may already be the peak. Therefore, we keep mid by setting right = mid instead of right = mid - 1.

Dry Run

Input

nums = [1,2,3,1]

Initial

left = 0
right = 3

Iteration 1

mid = 1

nums[mid] = 2
nums[mid + 1] = 3

2 < 3

Move right

left = mid + 1 = 2

Iteration 2

left = 2
right = 3

mid = 2

nums[mid] = 3
nums[mid + 1] = 1

3 > 1

Move left

right = mid = 2

Now

left = 2
right = 2

Loop ends.

Return index 2.

Time Complexity

O(log n)

Space Complexity

O(1)